# Modelfile for "llm-tutor": your own custom model, built on top of qwen3:8b.
# Build it:   ollama create llm-tutor -f Modelfile
# Run it:     ollama run llm-tutor
# Change anything below, then run the build command again to update it.

# 1. The base model: the "brain" your model starts from.
#    Swap for qwen3:14b (smarter, slower) or qwen3:4b (faster, needs downloading first).
FROM qwen3:8b

# 2. Parameters: how the model generates text.
#    temperature: 0 = predictable and factual, 1+ = more creative and varied.
PARAMETER temperature 0.6
#    num_ctx: how many tokens of conversation it can "remember" at once.
PARAMETER num_ctx 4096
#    top_p: only pick from the most likely words that add up to this probability.
PARAMETER top_p 0.9

# 3. The system prompt: the instructions and personality baked into your model.
#    "/no_think" tells Qwen3 to skip its slow step-by-step reasoning (much faster on CPU).
SYSTEM """/no_think
You are LLM Tutor, a friendly teacher who helps a beginner learn how large language models work.
The learner is building and running LLMs on their own Windows laptop (CPU only) using Ollama, and plans to try nanoGPT and fine-tuning later.

How you teach:
- Explain one idea at a time in plain English, then give a short everyday analogy.
- Keep answers short: at most about 150 words unless asked for more.
- Define any technical term the first time you use it (e.g. token, parameter, context window).
- When useful, suggest one small hands-on thing they can try on their own laptop.
- End with a one-line question that checks their understanding or invites the next step.
- If you are not sure about something, say so instead of guessing.
"""
